/* Pharmacology 1: General pharmacology */
Z("pharm1",0,"ph",["Routes of administration are the ways a drug enters the body — local, enteral (oral, sublingual, rectal) or parenteral (injections); transdermal therapeutic systems (patches) deliver drugs steadily through the skin.","Choosing a route is like choosing a delivery service: oral is cheap and slow, IV is express delivery straight to the blood, a skin patch is a slow drip feed for days.",["Parenteral: IV (100% bioavailability, immediate, for emergencies), IM, SC, intradermal, intrathecal, intra-arterial, intra-articular.","Parenteral advantages: rapid, reliable, for unconscious/vomiting patients, drugs destroyed orally (insulin, heparin); disadvantages: pain, sterility needed, costly, risk of infection/overdose, needs skilled person.","TTS (transdermal patch): drug reservoir + rate-controlling membrane + adhesive; e.g., nitroglycerin, fentanyl, nicotine, hyoscine, oestrogen, rivastigmine.","TTS advantages: steady levels, avoid first-pass, long duration, good compliance; disadvantages: skin irritation, only potent lipophilic drugs, slow onset."],"IV = 'Immediate, 100%'; TTS = 'Steady, skips liver'."],
"Routes of drug administration are classified as local (topical, deeper tissues, arterial supply), systemic enteral (oral, sublingual/buccal, rectal) and parenteral (injection — intravenous, intramuscular, subcutaneous, intradermal, intrathecal, intra-arterial, intra-articular, intraosseous; also inhalation, nasal, transdermal). Parenteral routes bypass the gut: intravenous gives 100% bioavailability and immediate action, allows titration and large volumes, and is suitable for irritants (diluted), emergencies and unconscious patients; drawbacks are no retreat once injected, thrombophlebitis, embolism, infection and need for sterility. Intramuscular suits moderate volumes, depot preparations and irritants unsuitable for SC; subcutaneous suits insulin, heparin and depot implants with slower absorption. General advantages of parenteral routes: rapid and predictable effect, avoidance of first-pass metabolism and GI destruction, usefulness in vomiting, unconscious or uncooperative patients; disadvantages: pain, need for asepsis and skilled personnel, higher cost, risk of infection, nerve injury and serious adverse reactions. Transdermal therapeutic systems are adhesive patches that deliver drug at a controlled rate through intact skin into systemic circulation over 1–7 days; they consist of a backing layer, drug reservoir, rate-controlling micropore membrane and adhesive layer. Examples: glyceryl trinitrate, hyoscine (motion sickness), fentanyl and buprenorphine (chronic pain), nicotine (smoking cessation), oestradiol, testosterone, clonidine, rivastigmine. Advantages: constant plasma levels, avoidance of first-pass metabolism, prolonged action, better compliance, easy termination by removing the patch; limitations: only potent, lipophilic, low-dose drugs, skin irritation, slow onset, cost.",
[["Parenteral routes",[["Route","Advantages","Disadvantages"],["Intravenous","Immediate, 100% bioavailability, titratable, large volumes","Irreversible, thrombophlebitis, infection, embolism"],["Intramuscular","Depot, moderate volumes, faster than SC","Painful, nerve injury, abscess"],["Subcutaneous","Self-administration (insulin), slow steady","Small volumes, irritants not allowed"],["Intradermal","Vaccines (BCG), skin tests","Very small volume"],["Intrathecal","Bypasses BBB (spinal anaesthesia)","Skill, infection risk"]]],
["Transdermal therapeutic system",["Backing layer → drug reservoir → rate-controlling membrane → adhesive","Examples: nitroglycerin, hyoscine, fentanyl, nicotine, oestrogen, rivastigmine","Advantages: steady levels, no first-pass, compliance","Limitations: potent lipophilic drugs only, irritation, slow onset"]]],
{c:"The route is selected by drug properties, urgency and patient factors; parenteral routes give speed and certainty, while transdermal systems give steady prolonged delivery."});

Z("pharm1",1,"ph",["Bioavailability is the fraction of an administered drug dose that reaches the systemic circulation unchanged; IV drugs have 100%.","Like pouring water through a leaky pipe: only part of what you pour (oral dose) reaches the tank (blood); the rest is lost to poor absorption and liver metabolism.",["Measured by AUC (area under plasma concentration–time curve): F = AUC oral / AUC IV × 100.","Factors: drug (solubility, particle size, formulation), first-pass metabolism, GI factors (pH, motility, food), disease (liver, gut), drug interactions.","Bioequivalence: two formulations with similar bioavailability (AUC, Cmax, Tmax within 80–125%).","Low oral bioavailability: lignocaine, nitroglycerin, propranolol, morphine (high first-pass)."],"F = AUC oral ÷ AUC IV."],
"Bioavailability (F) is the fraction (or percentage) of an administered dose of unchanged drug that reaches the systemic circulation and is available for action. By definition, an intravenous dose has 100% bioavailability. It is determined by comparing the area under the plasma concentration–time curve (AUC) after oral (or other route) administration with that after intravenous administration of the same dose: F = (AUC oral / AUC IV) × 100. Absolute bioavailability compares with IV; relative bioavailability compares two non-IV formulations. Factors affecting bioavailability: drug-related — physicochemical properties (lipid solubility, ionisation, molecular size), particle size and disintegration/dissolution rate, formulation (excipients, salt form, coating, sustained-release), and pharmaceutical manufacturing; patient-related — first-pass metabolism in gut wall and liver (lignocaine, propranolol, morphine, nitroglycerin), gastric emptying and intestinal motility, GI pH, food (increases griseofulvin, decreases ampicillin, tetracycline), GI disease, P-glycoprotein efflux, hepatic disease, drug interactions (antacids with tetracycline). Bioequivalence: two pharmaceutically equivalent preparations are bioequivalent if their rate and extent of absorption (Cmax, Tmax, AUC) do not differ significantly (90% CI within 80–125%); bioinequivalence is important for drugs with narrow therapeutic index (digoxin, phenytoin).",
[["Factors affecting bioavailability",[["Category","Examples"],["Drug","Solubility, particle size, formulation, dissolution"],["First-pass metabolism","Propranolol, lignocaine, morphine, nitrates"],["GI factors","pH, motility, food, P-glycoprotein"],["Disease","Liver cirrhosis (↑), malabsorption (↓)"],["Interactions","Antacids + tetracycline (chelation)"]]],
["Significance",["Determines oral dose vs IV dose","Bioequivalence for generic drugs","Narrow therapeutic index drugs need consistent brands"]]],
{c:"Bioavailability explains why oral and IV doses differ; ensuring bioequivalence is essential when substituting brands of critical drugs."});

Z("pharm1",2,"ph",["Many drugs travel in blood bound to plasma proteins (mainly albumin); only the free (unbound) part is active, can be metabolised and excreted.","Bound drug is like passengers sitting in a bus (albumin) — they cannot get off to act; only those standing at the stop (free drug) can do the work.",["Acidic drugs bind albumin (warfarin, phenytoin, sulphonamides, NSAIDs); basic drugs bind α1-acid glycoprotein (lignocaine, propranolol).","Bound fraction acts as a reservoir, prolongs action, not filtered by kidney, not dialysable.","Displacement interactions: one drug displaces another → ↑ free drug (sulphonamides displace bilirubin → kernicterus in neonates; aspirin displaces warfarin).","Hypoalbuminaemia (cirrhosis, nephrotic, malnutrition) → more free drug → toxicity (phenytoin)."],"Only free drug is 'Active, Metabolised, Excreted'."],
"Most drugs bind reversibly to plasma proteins: acidic drugs mainly to albumin (warfarin, phenytoin, sulphonamides, NSAIDs, valproate, tolbutamide, penicillins), basic drugs to α1-acid glycoprotein (lignocaine, propranolol, imipramine, quinidine). Only the free (unbound) fraction is pharmacologically active, diffuses into tissues, is metabolised and filtered by the kidney. Clinical significance: highly bound drugs (> 90%) have a small volume of distribution, are long-acting because bound drug serves as a reservoir releasing free drug as it is eliminated, are not removed by haemodialysis, and are not filtered at the glomerulus (though tubular secretion can remove them). Displacement interactions: drugs with higher affinity displace others from binding sites, transiently increasing free concentration — sulphonamides and ceftriaxone displace bilirubin causing kernicterus in neonates; aspirin, NSAIDs displace warfarin and sulphonylureas; valproate displaces phenytoin. Usually clinically important only when the displaced drug has a narrow therapeutic index and small Vd, or when displacement is combined with inhibition of metabolism. Hypoalbuminaemia (cirrhosis, nephrotic syndrome, malnutrition, old age) increases free fraction and toxicity — total drug levels of phenytoin may mislead. Binding is saturable and non-selective.",
[["Significance",["Bound drug inactive; free drug acts","Reservoir → longer duration","Restricted to vascular compartment (small Vd)","Not filtered, not dialysable","Displacement interactions","Altered in hypoalbuminaemia, uraemia, pregnancy"]],
["Examples of displacement",[["Displacer","Displaced","Effect"],["Sulphonamides, ceftriaxone","Bilirubin","Kernicterus in neonates"],["Aspirin, NSAIDs","Warfarin","Bleeding"],["Valproate","Phenytoin","Toxicity"],["Salicylates","Methotrexate","Toxicity"]]]],
{c:"Plasma protein binding influences distribution, duration and elimination; displacement and hypoalbuminaemia can precipitate toxicity of narrow-margin drugs."});

Z("pharm1",3,"ph",["Microsomal enzyme induction is when some drugs increase the amount of liver drug-metabolising enzymes (cytochrome P450), speeding up breakdown of themselves and other drugs.","Like a factory hiring more workers: drugs like rifampicin increase liver 'workers', so other drugs get processed (and removed) faster.",["Inducers: rifampicin, phenytoin, carbamazepine, phenobarbitone, griseofulvin, chronic alcohol, smoking, St John's wort.","Onset over days–weeks (new enzyme synthesis); reversible on stopping.","Consequences: failure of OC pills, warfarin, steroids, antiretrovirals; tolerance; ↑ toxic metabolites (paracetamol); vitamin D deficiency (osteomalacia).","Uses: phenobarbitone in neonatal jaundice and Crigler–Najjar II (induces glucuronyl transferase)."],"Inducers 'GPRS Cell Phone': Griseofulvin, Phenytoin, Rifampicin, Smoking, Carbamazepine, Phenobarbitone."],
"Microsomal enzyme induction is an increase in the amount and activity of drug-metabolising enzymes (mainly cytochrome P450 and glucuronyl transferase) in the liver endoplasmic reticulum, produced by certain drugs and chemicals on repeated administration through increased gene transcription (via nuclear receptors PXR, CAR, AhR). Common inducers: rifampicin (most potent), phenobarbitone and other barbiturates, phenytoin, carbamazepine, griseofulvin, chronic alcohol, cigarette smoking (CYP1A2), polycyclic hydrocarbons, St John's wort, nevirapine, efavirenz. Induction develops over days to 2–3 weeks and reverses gradually on withdrawal. Consequences: accelerated metabolism of the inducer itself (autoinduction — carbamazepine) and of other drugs causing therapeutic failure — oral contraceptives (pregnancy), warfarin (thrombosis; and toxicity if inducer stopped), corticosteroids, cyclosporine, antiretrovirals, theophylline, sulphonylureas; increased formation of toxic metabolites (paracetamol hepatotoxicity in alcoholics); increased metabolism of endogenous substances (vitamin D — osteomalacia with antiepileptics; cortisol; sex hormones); porphyria precipitation (barbiturates induce ALA synthase); tolerance. Therapeutic uses: phenobarbitone in neonatal hyperbilirubinaemia and Crigler–Najjar type II; to hasten clearance of certain drugs. Enzyme inhibition (opposite) by ketoconazole, erythromycin, cimetidine, ciprofloxacin, valproate, isoniazid, grapefruit juice causes toxicity of co-administered drugs.",
[["Inducers and inhibitors",[["Inducers","Inhibitors"],["Rifampicin","Ketoconazole, itraconazole"],["Phenytoin, carbamazepine, phenobarbitone","Erythromycin, clarithromycin"],["Griseofulvin","Cimetidine"],["Chronic alcohol, smoking","Ciprofloxacin, isoniazid"],["St John's wort","Valproate, grapefruit juice, ritonavir"]]],
["Consequences of induction",["OCP failure","Reduced warfarin, steroid, ARV effect","Paracetamol toxicity ↑","Osteomalacia (vitamin D)","Porphyria precipitation","Autoinduction (carbamazepine)"]]],
{c:"Enzyme induction is a major mechanism of drug interactions; dose adjustment or alternative drugs are needed when inducers are started or stopped."});

Z("pharm1",4,"ph",["First-pass metabolism is the breakdown of an orally given drug in the gut wall and liver before it reaches the systemic circulation, reducing its bioavailability.","An oral drug must pass through the liver's 'customs check' first; some drugs are mostly confiscated there before entering the body.",["Sites: gut wall, liver (main), lungs.","High first-pass drugs: nitroglycerin, lignocaine, propranolol, morphine, verapamil, testosterone, isoprenaline.","Consequences: low oral bioavailability, large oral vs IV dose difference, individual variation; liver disease ↑ bioavailability.","Avoid by sublingual, transdermal, rectal (partly), parenteral, inhalational routes."],"Bypass first-pass: 'Sublingual, Skin, Shot (IV), Sniff (inhaled)'."],
"First-pass (presystemic) metabolism is the metabolism of a drug during its passage from the site of absorption into the systemic circulation, mainly in the gut wall and liver (also lungs for some drugs). After oral administration, drug absorbed from the gut reaches the liver via the portal vein; drugs with high hepatic extraction are largely metabolised before reaching systemic circulation. Drugs with high first-pass metabolism: nitroglycerin (> 90%; given sublingually), lignocaine (not given orally), isoprenaline, propranolol, morphine, pethidine, verapamil, salbutamol, testosterone, imipramine, hydrocortisone, aspirin (partial), levodopa. Consequences: low and variable oral bioavailability; oral dose much higher than parenteral dose (propranolol 40 mg oral vs 1 mg IV); inter-individual variation; increased bioavailability and toxicity in liver cirrhosis or with enzyme inhibitors and portosystemic shunts; formation of active metabolites (prodrugs such as enalapril). Avoided by sublingual/buccal, transdermal, inhalational and parenteral routes; rectal route avoids it partly (lower rectal veins).",
[["Examples",[["Drug","Consequence"],["Nitroglycerin","Given sublingually/transdermally"],["Lignocaine","Not effective orally"],["Propranolol","Oral dose ≫ IV dose"],["Morphine","Oral bioavailability ~25%"],["Testosterone","Given IM/transdermal"]]]],
{c:"First-pass metabolism reduces oral bioavailability of many drugs; route selection and dose adjustment, especially in liver disease, overcome its effects."});

Z("pharm1",5,"ph",["In first-order kinetics a constant fraction of drug is eliminated per unit time (most drugs); in zero-order kinetics a constant amount is eliminated per unit time because the elimination enzymes are saturated.","First-order: a fixed percentage leaves every hour, like a bank interest in reverse. Zero-order: a fixed number leaves every hour, like a single cashier who can serve only so many customers no matter how long the queue.",["First-order: rate ∝ concentration, constant t½, steady state in 4–5 half-lives, linear kinetics.","Zero-order: rate constant regardless of concentration, t½ increases with dose, small dose increase → large rise in levels (toxicity).","Zero-order (saturation) drugs: alcohol, phenytoin, aspirin (high doses), theophylline, heparin (high dose) — 'PAT'.","Phenytoin follows mixed (Michaelis–Menten) kinetics — needs therapeutic drug monitoring."],"Zero-order: 'Alcohol, Phenytoin, Aspirin, Theophylline' — fixed amount out."],
"Drug elimination follows kinetics determined by the capacity of elimination processes. First-order (exponential, linear) kinetics: the rate of elimination is directly proportional to plasma concentration — a constant fraction (not amount) of drug is eliminated per unit time; clearance and half-life remain constant irrespective of dose; plasma concentration–time curve is exponential (linear on semilog plot); steady state is reached after 4–5 half-lives; most drugs at therapeutic doses follow first-order kinetics because enzyme and transporter capacity far exceeds drug concentration. Zero-order (saturation, non-linear) kinetics: elimination mechanisms are saturated, so a constant amount of drug is eliminated per unit time regardless of concentration; half-life increases with dose; plasma concentration falls linearly; small dose increments can cause disproportionate rise in plasma levels and toxicity; steady state may not be reached. Examples: ethanol (always zero-order), phenytoin, high-dose aspirin/salicylates, theophylline, tolbutamide, warfarin and heparin at high doses. Many drugs show Michaelis–Menten (mixed) kinetics: first-order at low concentrations and zero-order when enzymes are saturated (phenytoin — therapeutic range near saturation, requiring therapeutic drug monitoring).",
[["Comparison",[["Feature","First-order","Zero-order"],["Elimination","Constant fraction/time","Constant amount/time"],["Rate vs concentration","Proportional","Independent"],["Half-life","Constant","Increases with dose"],["Clearance","Constant","Decreases with dose"],["Plot (linear axes)","Exponential curve","Straight line"],["Dose–level relation","Proportional","Disproportionate rise"],["Examples","Most drugs","Alcohol, phenytoin, high-dose aspirin, theophylline"]]]],
{c:"Recognising zero-order (saturable) drugs is clinically important because small dose changes can cause large increases in plasma concentration and toxicity."});

Z("pharm1",6,"ph",["Plasma half-life (t½) is the time taken for the plasma concentration of a drug to fall to half; it decides dosing interval and time to reach steady state.","Every half-life, half of what's left disappears: 100 → 50 → 25 → 12.5 → 6.25 — after about 4–5 half-lives the drug is almost gone.",["For first-order drugs: t½ = 0.693 × Vd / CL.","Steady state is reached in 4–5 half-lives; drug is ~97% eliminated after 5 half-lives.","Determines dosing frequency; long t½ drugs need loading dose (digoxin, amiodarone).","Prolonged by renal/hepatic failure, enzyme inhibitors, elderly; shortened by enzyme inducers."],"t½ = 0.693 × Vd / CL; 4–5 half-lives to steady state or washout."],
"Plasma half-life is the time taken for the plasma concentration of a drug (or the amount in the body) to be reduced to half. For drugs following first-order kinetics it is constant and related to volume of distribution and clearance: t½ = 0.693 × Vd / CL. After one half-life 50% is eliminated, after two 75%, three 87.5%, four 93.75% and five about 97%; so for practical purposes a drug is eliminated in 4–5 half-lives. Similarly, on repeated dosing at a constant rate, steady-state plasma concentration is reached in 4–5 half-lives, irrespective of dose. Biological effect half-life may differ from plasma half-life for hit-and-run drugs (reserpine, omeprazole, aspirin on platelets). Significance: determines dosing interval (short t½ drugs need frequent dosing or sustained-release forms), time to steady state and to washout, need for loading dose (drugs with long t½ — digoxin, amiodarone, chloroquine), duration of action, and time for complete elimination before switching drugs or surgery. Factors altering half-life: renal failure (aminoglycosides, digoxin), hepatic disease, heart failure (reduced hepatic blood flow), age (neonates, elderly), enzyme induction or inhibition, plasma protein binding, Vd changes. In zero-order kinetics t½ is not constant and increases with dose.",
[["Significance",["Dosing interval","Time to steady state (4–5 t½)","Time for elimination (4–5 t½)","Need for loading dose (long t½)","Dose adjustment in renal/hepatic failure"]],
["Examples",[["Drug","Approx t½"],["Penicillin G","30 min"],["Paracetamol","2–3 h"],["Digoxin","40 h"],["Phenobarbitone","90 h"],["Amiodarone","3–8 weeks"]]]],
{c:"Half-life links clearance and distribution to dosing schedules; it guides frequency, loading doses and adjustment in organ failure."});

Z("pharm1",7,"ph",["G-protein coupled receptors are the largest family of drug receptors; they cross the cell membrane seven times and transmit signals through G proteins to second messengers like cAMP, IP3 and Ca²⁺.","A doorbell (receptor) outside the house rings an intercom (G protein) inside, which then switches on lights and machinery (second messengers).",["Structure: single polypeptide with 7 transmembrane helices; ligand binds outside, G protein (α, β, γ) inside.","Gs → ↑ adenylyl cyclase → ↑ cAMP (β-adrenergic, H2, D1, glucagon); Gi → ↓ cAMP, opens K⁺ channels (M2, α2, D2, opioid).","Gq → phospholipase C → IP3 (↑ Ca²⁺) + DAG (protein kinase C) (α1, M1, M3, H1, AT1, 5-HT2).","Response in seconds; drug targets: β-blockers, antihistamines, opioids, antipsychotics, triptans."],"Gs Stimulates cAMP; Gi Inhibits; Gq makes IP3 + DAG (Ca²⁺)."],
"G-protein coupled receptors (GPCRs, metabotropic receptors) are the largest family of cell-surface receptors and drug targets (about a third of all drugs). Each is a single polypeptide chain with seven α-helical transmembrane segments (serpentine receptor), an extracellular N-terminus and ligand-binding pocket, and intracellular loops coupling to heterotrimeric G proteins (α, β, γ subunits). On agonist binding, the receptor changes conformation, causing the Gα subunit to exchange GDP for GTP and dissociate from βγ; Gα-GTP and βγ regulate effectors until intrinsic GTPase activity hydrolyses GTP and restores the inactive state. Major G proteins and pathways: Gs — stimulates adenylyl cyclase, raising cAMP, activating protein kinase A (β1, β2, β3 adrenergic, D1, H2, V2, glucagon, FSH, LH, TSH, PGE2/PGI2 receptors); Gi/Go — inhibits adenylyl cyclase, opens K⁺ channels (hyperpolarisation), closes Ca²⁺ channels (M2, α2, D2, μ/κ/δ opioid, GABA-B, 5-HT1, CB1); Gq — activates phospholipase C-β, hydrolysing PIP2 into IP3 (releases Ca²⁺ from ER) and DAG (activates protein kinase C) (α1, M1, M3, M5, H1, AT1, V1, 5-HT2, ETA receptors); G12/13 — Rho activation. Other effectors: ion channels, phospholipase A2, cGMP phosphodiesterase (transducin in retina). Response latency is seconds; signal amplification occurs. Regulation: desensitisation through GRK phosphorylation and β-arrestin binding, internalisation, downregulation. Clinical examples: β-agonists and blockers, muscarinic agonists/antagonists, antihistamines, opioids, antipsychotics (D2), triptans (5-HT1B/1D), angiotensin receptor blockers. Cholera toxin (Gs ADP-ribosylation) and pertussis toxin (Gi) act on G proteins.",
[["G proteins and receptors",[["G protein","Effector","Second messenger","Receptor examples"],["Gs","↑ Adenylyl cyclase","↑ cAMP","β1, β2, D1, H2, V2"],["Gi","↓ Adenylyl cyclase; ↑ K⁺ channels","↓ cAMP","α2, M2, D2, opioid, GABA-B"],["Gq","↑ Phospholipase C","IP3 (Ca²⁺), DAG (PKC)","α1, M1, M3, H1, AT1, 5-HT2"]]],
["Receptor families (comparison)",[["Type","Example","Response time"],["Ligand-gated ion channel","Nicotinic, GABA-A","Milliseconds"],["GPCR","Muscarinic, adrenergic","Seconds"],["Enzyme-linked (kinase)","Insulin","Minutes"],["Nuclear receptor","Steroid, thyroid","Hours"]]]],
{c:"GPCRs translate extracellular signals into intracellular second messengers and are targets for a large share of clinically used drugs."});

Z("pharm1",8,"ph",["Nuclear receptors are intracellular receptors for lipid-soluble hormones (steroids, thyroid hormone, vitamin D, retinoids) that, once activated, bind DNA and change gene transcription.","The hormone walks inside the house (cell), meets its receptor in the living room or nucleus, and together they edit the instruction manual (DNA).",["Ligands: glucocorticoids, mineralocorticoids, oestrogen, progesterone, androgens, thyroid hormone, vitamin D, retinoic acid, PPAR (glitazones, fibrates).","Structure: ligand-binding domain, DNA-binding domain (zinc fingers), transactivation domain.","Act on hormone response elements → new protein synthesis; slow onset (hours), long-lasting effects.","Examples of drugs: corticosteroids, tamoxifen (SERM), spironolactone, levothyroxine, pioglitazone (PPAR-γ), fenofibrate (PPAR-α)."],"Nuclear receptors = 'slow but long': hours to days."],
"Nuclear receptors are intracellular ligand-activated transcription factors that mediate the actions of lipophilic hormones and drugs. Ligands: glucocorticoids, mineralocorticoids, oestrogens, progestins, androgens (class I — cytoplasmic receptors bound to heat-shock proteins, which dissociate on ligand binding, dimerise and translocate to the nucleus), and thyroid hormone, vitamin D3, retinoic acid, peroxisome proliferator-activated receptors (PPAR α, γ, δ), liver X receptor, pregnane X receptor (class II — located in the nucleus, often as heterodimers with retinoid X receptor). Structure: N-terminal transactivation domain, central DNA-binding domain with two zinc fingers, hinge region, and C-terminal ligand-binding domain. The ligand–receptor complex binds specific DNA sequences called hormone response elements in promoter regions, recruiting coactivators or corepressors to increase or decrease transcription of target genes, leading to synthesis of new proteins. Hence the response has a lag period of 30 minutes to several hours and persists after the drug is gone. Non-genomic rapid actions also exist. Drug examples: corticosteroids (anti-inflammatory via induction of lipocortin, repression of NF-κB), tamoxifen and raloxifene (selective oestrogen receptor modulators), mifepristone (progesterone receptor antagonist), spironolactone (mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist), levothyroxine, calcitriol, isotretinoin and tretinoin (RAR), thiazolidinediones (PPAR-γ agonists), fibrates (PPAR-α agonists), rifampicin and phenobarbitone (via PXR/CAR → enzyme induction).",
[["Ligands and drugs",[["Receptor","Endogenous ligand","Drug"],["GR","Cortisol","Prednisolone, dexamethasone"],["MR","Aldosterone","Spironolactone (antagonist)"],["ER","Oestradiol","Tamoxifen, raloxifene"],["PR","Progesterone","Mifepristone"],["TR","T3","Levothyroxine"],["VDR","Calcitriol","Calcitriol"],["RAR","Retinoic acid","Tretinoin"],["PPAR-γ / α","Fatty acids","Pioglitazone / fenofibrate"]]]],
{c:"Nuclear receptors regulate gene expression; their drugs act slowly but produce prolonged effects, which explains the time course of steroid and thyroid therapy."});

Z("pharm1",9,"ph",["Pharmacokinetics is what the body does to the drug — Absorption, Distribution, Metabolism and Excretion (ADME).","A drug's journey through the body: getting in (absorption), travelling around (distribution), being changed (metabolism) and leaving (excretion).",["Absorption: passive diffusion (lipid-soluble, non-ionised), carrier transport, filtration, pinocytosis; affected by pH, formulation, route.","Distribution: volume of distribution (Vd), plasma protein binding, tissue binding, barriers (BBB, placenta).","Metabolism (mainly liver): phase I (oxidation — CYP450, reduction, hydrolysis) and phase II (conjugation — glucuronidation, acetylation, sulphation); prodrugs.","Excretion: kidney (filtration, secretion, reabsorption — pH-dependent), bile, lungs, milk; clearance and half-life."],"ADME = 'A Drug Moves Everywhere'."],
"Pharmacokinetics is the quantitative study of drug movement in, through and out of the body — absorption, distribution, metabolism (biotransformation) and excretion (ADME). Absorption: movement from site of administration into circulation by passive diffusion (most drugs; depends on lipid solubility, ionisation — weak acids absorbed better in acidic stomach, weak bases in alkaline intestine — molecular size), carrier-mediated facilitated diffusion and active transport (levodopa, iron), filtration, and endocytosis; influenced by formulation, gastric emptying, food, blood flow, surface area, first-pass metabolism; quantified as bioavailability. Distribution: drugs reach tissues according to lipid solubility, ionisation, blood flow, protein binding and tissue affinity; apparent volume of distribution (Vd = dose / plasma concentration) — small for highly protein-bound drugs (warfarin), large for tissue-sequestered drugs (chloroquine, digoxin); barriers — blood–brain barrier, placenta; redistribution (thiopentone). Metabolism: mostly in liver microsomal enzymes; phase I (functionalisation — oxidation by cytochrome P450, reduction, hydrolysis) and phase II (synthetic conjugation — glucuronidation, acetylation (genetic slow/fast acetylators), sulphation, methylation, glutathione conjugation) produce more water-soluble, usually inactive metabolites; prodrugs are activated (levodopa, enalapril, clopidogrel); enzyme induction and inhibition; Hofmann elimination (atracurium). Excretion: kidney — glomerular filtration of free drug, active tubular secretion (penicillin, probenecid blocks), passive tubular reabsorption (pH-dependent; alkalinising urine enhances excretion of weak acids like aspirin, phenobarbitone); bile and faeces with enterohepatic circulation; lungs (anaesthetics, alcohol); saliva, sweat, breast milk. Kinetic parameters: clearance (CL = rate of elimination / plasma concentration), half-life, first- and zero-order kinetics, steady state, loading and maintenance doses, therapeutic drug monitoring.",
[["ADME summary",[["Process","Key concepts","Examples"],["Absorption","Lipid solubility, pH, bioavailability, first-pass","Aspirin absorbed in stomach"],["Distribution","Vd, protein binding, BBB, placenta, redistribution","Thiopentone redistribution"],["Metabolism","Phase I (CYP450), phase II (conjugation), induction, inhibition, prodrugs","Rifampicin induces; enalapril prodrug"],["Excretion","Filtration, secretion, reabsorption, urine pH, biliary","Alkaline diuresis in aspirin poisoning"]]],
["Key parameters",["Bioavailability (F)","Volume of distribution (Vd)","Clearance (CL)","Half-life (t½ = 0.693 Vd/CL)","Loading dose = Vd × target Cp; maintenance dose = CL × target Cp"]]],
{c:"Pharmacokinetics explains how dose, route and patient factors determine drug concentration at the site of action and forms the basis of rational dosing."});

Z("pharm1",10,"ph",["Pharmacodynamics is what the drug does to the body — its mechanism of action, receptors, dose–response relationship and effects.","If pharmacokinetics is the drug's journey, pharmacodynamics is what it does on arrival: which locks (receptors) it opens or blocks and how strong the effect is.",["Mechanisms: receptors (agonist, antagonist, partial, inverse agonist), enzymes (aspirin–COX), ion channels (local anaesthetics), transporters (SSRIs), physical/chemical (antacids, osmotic diuretics).","Receptor types: ligand-gated ion channels, GPCRs, enzyme-linked (kinase), nuclear receptors.","Dose–response: potency (EC50), efficacy (Emax); therapeutic index (LD50/ED50).","Antagonism: competitive (surmountable — atropine), non-competitive, physiological, chemical."],"Potency = how little drug needed; Efficacy = how big the maximum effect."],
"Pharmacodynamics is the study of biochemical and physiological effects of drugs and their mechanisms of action. Drugs act by: physical actions (osmotic diuretics, bulk laxatives, activated charcoal adsorption), chemical actions (antacids, chelating agents, protamine), enzymes (stimulation or inhibition — aspirin irreversibly inhibits COX, neostigmine inhibits cholinesterase, ACE inhibitors, allopurinol), ion channels (blockers — local anaesthetics on Na⁺ channels, calcium channel blockers; openers — minoxidil on K⁺ channels), transporters (digoxin on Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase, omeprazole on proton pump, SSRIs on serotonin transporter, furosemide on Na⁺-K⁺-2Cl⁻ cotransporter), antibodies and receptors. Receptors are macromolecules with which drugs interact to produce effects; families — ligand-gated ion channels (nicotinic, GABA-A; milliseconds), G-protein coupled receptors (muscarinic, adrenergic; seconds), enzyme-linked/kinase receptors (insulin, cytokine receptors; minutes), nuclear receptors (steroids, thyroid; hours). Drug–receptor terms: affinity (ability to bind), intrinsic activity (ability to activate); agonist (full activation), partial agonist (submaximal — buprenorphine, pindolol), antagonist (blocks — atropine, propranolol), inverse agonist (opposite effect — β-carbolines on benzodiazepine site). Dose–response relationship: graded (log dose–response curve is sigmoid) and quantal; potency (amount of drug needed for a given effect; EC50) versus efficacy (maximal effect attainable; Emax — loop diuretics more efficacious than thiazides). Therapeutic index = median lethal dose / median effective dose (LD50/ED50; or TD50/ED50); therapeutic window. Drug antagonism: physical, chemical, physiological/functional (adrenaline vs histamine), receptor — competitive reversible (parallel rightward shift, surmountable), irreversible/non-equilibrium (phenoxybenzamine), non-competitive. Synergism: additive and supra-additive (potentiation). Other concepts: selectivity, specificity, receptor regulation (up/down-regulation, desensitisation, tachyphylaxis), placebo effect.",
[["Mechanisms of drug action",[["Mechanism","Example"],["Receptors","Atropine (muscarinic), propranolol (β)"],["Enzymes","Aspirin (COX), neostigmine (AChE), captopril (ACE)"],["Ion channels","Lignocaine (Na⁺), nifedipine (Ca²⁺)"],["Transporters","Digoxin (Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase), fluoxetine (SERT), omeprazole (H⁺/K⁺-ATPase)"],["Physical","Mannitol, activated charcoal"],["Chemical","Antacids, chelators, protamine"]]],
["Agonists and antagonists",[["Term","Affinity","Intrinsic activity","Example"],["Full agonist","Yes","1","Morphine"],["Partial agonist","Yes","0–1","Buprenorphine"],["Antagonist","Yes","0","Naloxone"],["Inverse agonist","Yes","−1","β-carbolines"]]],
["Potency vs efficacy",["Potency: dose required (position of curve on x-axis)","Efficacy: maximal response (height of curve)","Efficacy more important clinically"]]],
{c:"Pharmacodynamics explains how drugs produce effects through specific targets and how dose relates to response, guiding selection and safety of drugs."});

Z("pharm1",11,"ph",["Fixed-dose combinations (FDCs) are two or more drugs combined in a single tablet in fixed proportions; some are rational and helpful, many irrational ones are banned in India.","A combo meal can be convenient if all items are needed and match; it becomes wasteful or harmful when you're forced to take items you don't need, in wrong amounts.",["Rational FDCs: anti-TB (HRZE), antiretroviral (TLD), co-trimoxazole, levodopa + carbidopa, amoxicillin + clavulanate, ACT for malaria, iron + folic acid, OCPs.","Advantages: convenience, better compliance, synergism, reduced side effects, delay of resistance (TB, HIV, malaria), lower cost.","Disadvantages: inflexible dosing, different half-lives, unknown culprit in ADR, irrational combinations, increased cost/unnecessary drugs.","India: many irrational FDCs banned (2016, 2018); criteria — each drug contributes, compatible kinetics, no new toxicity."],"Rational FDC: 'TB, HIV, Malaria, Parkinson, Co-trimoxazole'."],
"Fixed-dose combinations are formulations containing two or more active drugs in a fixed ratio in a single dosage form. Rational FDCs: combinations for which each component contributes to efficacy or safety, components have compatible pharmacokinetics (similar half-lives and timing), there is no new toxicity, and the combination is proven by evidence. Examples: anti-tubercular FDCs (rifampicin + isoniazid + pyrazinamide + ethambutol) — prevent monotherapy and resistance; antiretrovirals (tenofovir + lamivudine + dolutegravir); artemisinin-based combinations (artemether + lumefantrine); co-trimoxazole (sequential blockade); levodopa + carbidopa (peripheral decarboxylase inhibition); amoxicillin + clavulanic acid (β-lactamase inhibition); oral contraceptives (oestrogen + progestin); ferrous sulphate + folic acid; ORS; antihypertensive combinations (ACE inhibitor/ARB + thiazide or amlodipine); metformin + sitagliptin; inhaled steroid + long-acting β2 agonist. Advantages: convenience and improved adherence (fewer pills), synergistic or additive effects, reduced adverse effects by using lower doses or counteracting effects, prevention of resistance, reduced cost and dispensing errors. Disadvantages: fixed ratio prevents individual dose titration, mismatch in half-lives, difficulty in identifying the drug causing ADR, contraindication to one component excludes all, irrational FDCs increase cost and adverse effects and promote antimicrobial resistance (e.g., antibiotic + antidiarrhoeal, multiple analgesics, cough syrups with mixed actions). In India, the Kokate committee (2016) led to banning of several hundred irrational FDCs; CDSCO approval is mandatory for new FDCs.",
[["Rational FDC examples",[["FDC","Rationale"],["HRZE (anti-TB)","Prevents resistance, compliance"],["TLD (HIV)","Potent regimen, one pill"],["Artemether + lumefantrine","Prevents resistance"],["Levodopa + carbidopa","↓ peripheral decarboxylation, ↓ dose, ↓ nausea"],["Amoxicillin + clavulanate","β-lactamase inhibition"],["Co-trimoxazole","Sequential blockade, synergy"]]],
["Advantages vs disadvantages",[["Advantages","Disadvantages"],["Compliance","No dose titration"],["Synergy","Mismatched kinetics"],["↓ Side effects","Unclear ADR source"],["↓ Resistance","Irrational FDCs, cost"]]]],
{c:"FDCs are valuable when rational and evidence-based, but irrational combinations increase risk and cost and are being removed by regulators."});

Z("pharm1",12,"ph",["Many factors change how a person responds to the same drug: body size, age, sex, genetics, disease, diet, other drugs, and psychological factors.","The same key may open different doors differently: a child, an elderly person, a slow acetylator or a patient with kidney failure each handle a drug in their own way.",["Body weight and surface area: dose calculation, especially children (Young's, Clark's formulae, BSA).","Age: neonates — immature liver/kidney (chloramphenicol grey baby); elderly — ↓ renal/hepatic function, sensitivity (benzodiazepines).","Genetics (pharmacogenetics): slow acetylators (INH neuropathy), G6PD deficiency (haemolysis with primaquine), pseudocholinesterase deficiency (succinylcholine apnoea), HLA-B*1502 (carbamazepine SJS).","Disease (renal, hepatic, cardiac), pregnancy, route, timing, tolerance, drug interactions, placebo, environment."],"Factors 'BAGS DRIP': Body weight, Age, Genetics, Sex, Disease, Route, Interactions, Psychology."],
"Individuals vary widely in response to drugs; factors modifying drug action include: (1) body size — weight and body surface area determine dose (mg/kg; BSA for anticancer drugs); obesity alters Vd of lipophilic drugs; (2) age — neonates and infants have immature hepatic metabolism and renal excretion, higher body water and permeable BBB (chloramphenicol grey baby syndrome, sulphonamide kernicterus, tetracycline teeth staining); children's dose by Young's (age/(age+12) × adult dose), Clark's (weight in lb/150 × adult dose) or BSA formulae; elderly have reduced renal and hepatic clearance, altered body composition, increased sensitivity (CNS depressants, anticoagulants), polypharmacy and adherence problems; (3) sex — pregnancy, lactation, menstruation; hormonal differences; (4) species and race (ethnic differences — e.g., Asians more sensitive to warfarin and carbamazepine SJS); (5) genetic factors (pharmacogenetics) — acetylator status (isoniazid neuropathy in slow acetylators, hydralazine/procainamide lupus), G6PD deficiency (haemolysis with primaquine, dapsone, sulphonamides), atypical pseudocholinesterase (prolonged succinylcholine apnoea), malignant hyperthermia (halothane + succinylcholine; RYR1), CYP2C9/VKORC1 (warfarin dose), CYP2C19 (clopidogrel non-response), HLA-B*5701 (abacavir hypersensitivity), HLA-B*1502 (carbamazepine SJS in Asians), TPMT (azathioprine toxicity), acute intermittent porphyria (barbiturates); (6) route of administration; (7) environmental factors and time of administration — food, smoking (enzyme induction), alcohol, circadian rhythm (chronopharmacology — steroids in morning); (8) psychological factors — placebo and nocebo effects; (9) pathological states — renal failure (aminoglycosides, digoxin accumulation), liver disease (reduced metabolism, hepatic encephalopathy with sedatives), congestive heart failure, thyroid disease, GI disease; (10) other drugs — interactions (pharmacokinetic and pharmacodynamic); (11) cumulation; (12) tolerance — natural or acquired, cross-tolerance, tachyphylaxis; (13) dose and formulation.",
[["Factors and examples",[["Factor","Example"],["Age — neonates","Grey baby syndrome (chloramphenicol)"],["Age — elderly","Increased sensitivity to benzodiazepines, digoxin"],["Pregnancy","Teratogenicity, altered kinetics"],["Genetic","Slow acetylators — INH neuropathy; G6PD — haemolysis"],["Renal disease","Aminoglycoside, digoxin toxicity"],["Liver disease","Morphine, benzodiazepine sensitivity"],["Interactions","Rifampicin ↓ OCP effect"],["Psychological","Placebo response"],["Tolerance","Morphine, nitrates"]]],
["Paediatric dose formulae",["Young's: child dose = age / (age + 12) × adult dose","Clark's: weight (lb) / 150 × adult dose","BSA: BSA (m²) / 1.73 × adult dose"]]],
{i:"Pharmacological responses vary between individuals and within the same individual over time; recognising modifying factors is the basis of individualised therapy.",c:"Doses and drug choice must be tailored to age, weight, genetics, disease state and concurrent drugs to maximise benefit and minimise harm."});

Z("pharm1",13,"ph",["Rational use of medicines means patients receive the right drug for their clinical need, in the right dose, for the right duration, at the lowest cost to them and the community (WHO).","Like using the right tool for the job — not a hammer for every problem, and not two hammers when one will do.",["Steps (WHO): define problem, specify objective, choose P-drug (efficacy, safety, suitability, cost), prescribe correctly, inform patient, monitor.","Essential medicines list (EML/NLEM) and standard treatment guidelines support rationality.","Irrational prescribing: polypharmacy, unnecessary antibiotics and injections, irrational FDCs, brand-name prescribing, inadequate dose/duration, self-medication.","Consequences: ADRs, AMR, cost, poor outcomes; promoted through education, generic prescribing, stewardship, audits."],"Rational use: 'Right drug, Right dose, Right duration, Right cost, Right patient'."],
"Rational use of medicines (WHO, 1985) requires that patients receive medications appropriate to their clinical needs, in doses that meet their individual requirements, for an adequate period of time, and at the lowest cost to them and their community. Process of rational prescribing (WHO Guide to Good Prescribing): define the patient's problem (diagnosis); specify the therapeutic objective; verify suitability of personal drug (P-drug) chosen on efficacy, safety, suitability and cost; write a complete legible prescription (generic name, dose, frequency, duration); give information, instructions and warnings to the patient; monitor and stop treatment when appropriate. Tools: National List of Essential Medicines (NLEM, India), WHO Model List of Essential Medicines, standard treatment guidelines, hospital formularies, drug and therapeutics committees, drug information centres, prescription audits. Common irrational practices: polypharmacy; use of drugs without indication (antibiotics for viral infections and diarrhoea); inappropriate choice; wrong dose, route or duration; excessive use of injections, vitamins, tonics and expensive brands; irrational FDCs; self-medication and over-the-counter antibiotics; failure to follow guidelines; poor patient adherence. Causes: lack of knowledge, diagnostic uncertainty, patient demand, pharmaceutical promotion, financial incentives, weak regulation. Consequences: adverse drug reactions, antimicrobial resistance, treatment failure, increased cost, loss of patient confidence. Strategies: education of prescribers and public, generic prescribing (Jan Aushadhi), essential medicines concept, guidelines, regulation, antimicrobial stewardship, prescription audit and feedback, pharmacovigilance.",
[["Steps of rational prescribing",["Define the problem","Specify objective","Choose P-drug (efficacy, safety, suitability, cost)","Write prescription","Inform the patient","Monitor/stop"]],
["Irrational practices",["Polypharmacy","Antibiotics for viral illness","Unnecessary injections, tonics","Irrational FDCs","Self-medication","Inadequate dose/duration"]]],
{c:"Rational use of medicines improves outcomes, reduces costs and limits resistance; essential medicines lists, guidelines and prescriber education are its pillars."});

Z("pharm1",14,"ph",["Drug tolerance is a reduced response to a drug after repeated use, so higher doses are needed for the same effect.","The body adapts to a repeated visitor: it gets used to the drug, either by processing it faster or by turning down its sensitivity.",["Types: natural (innate — species, racial), acquired (repeated use), cross-tolerance (between related drugs — alcohol and benzodiazepines), tachyphylaxis (rapid tolerance — ephedrine, nitrates).","Mechanisms: pharmacokinetic (enzyme induction — barbiturates), pharmacodynamic (receptor downregulation — opioids, β-agonists), physiological compensation.","Tolerance develops to some effects but not others (morphine: tolerance to analgesia and euphoria, not miosis and constipation).","Clinical relevance: dose escalation, nitrate-free interval, drug dependence."],"Tachyphylaxis = 'Tachy' (fast) tolerance — ephedrine, nitrates."],
"Tolerance is the requirement of a higher dose of a drug to produce a given response, i.e., diminished responsiveness after repeated exposure. Types: natural (innate) tolerance — inherent lower sensitivity of a species or individual (rabbits to atropine due to atropinase; black races to mydriatics); acquired tolerance — develops on repeated use (opioids, barbiturates, alcohol, nitrates, benzodiazepines); tolerance may develop to some actions and not others (morphine — analgesia, euphoria, respiratory depression but not miosis and constipation; phenobarbitone — sedation but not antiepileptic effect); cross-tolerance — tolerance to pharmacologically related drugs (alcohol, barbiturates, benzodiazepines, general anaesthetics; morphine and other opioids); tachyphylaxis (acute tolerance) — rapid development after a few doses due to depletion of mediator stores or receptor desensitisation (ephedrine, tyramine, amphetamine — depletion of noradrenaline; nitrates; DDAVP; nicotine). Mechanisms: pharmacokinetic (dispositional) tolerance — increased metabolism by enzyme induction (barbiturates, alcohol, carbamazepine autoinduction); pharmacodynamic (cellular) tolerance — receptor downregulation and desensitisation (β2 agonists, opioids — μ receptor uncoupling and internalisation), adaptation of signalling pathways (cAMP upregulation with opioids), depletion of mediators; physiological (homeostatic) compensation (sodium retention with antihypertensives). Drug resistance refers to reduced effect of chemotherapeutic agents on microbes or tumours. Clinical implications: need for dose escalation, risk of dependence and withdrawal, overdose when tolerance is lost after abstinence, nitrate-free interval of 8–12 hours to prevent nitrate tolerance.",
[["Types",[["Type","Example"],["Natural","Rabbits to atropine"],["Acquired","Opioids, barbiturates, alcohol"],["Cross-tolerance","Alcohol ↔ benzodiazepines; morphine ↔ pethidine"],["Tachyphylaxis","Ephedrine, tyramine, nitrates"]]],
["Mechanisms",["Pharmacokinetic: enzyme induction","Pharmacodynamic: receptor downregulation/desensitisation","Mediator depletion (tachyphylaxis)","Physiological compensation"]]],
{c:"Tolerance reduces drug effect with repeated use through kinetic and dynamic adaptations; it guides dosing schedules and is linked to dependence."});

Z("pharm1",15,"ph",["Teratogenicity is the ability of a drug to cause birth defects when taken during pregnancy, especially during organ formation (3–8 weeks after conception).","The embryo is a building under construction; drugs during the framework stage (organogenesis) can permanently distort the structure.",["Critical period: organogenesis (day 18–55); earlier — all-or-none effect; later — growth/functional defects.","Known teratogens: thalidomide (phocomelia), isotretinoin, valproate (neural tube defects), phenytoin (fetal hydantoin), warfarin (nasal hypoplasia), ACE inhibitors (renal), tetracyclines (teeth), methotrexate, lithium (Ebstein anomaly), DES (vaginal adenocarcinoma), alcohol (FAS).","Safe options: penicillins, cephalosporins, insulin, methyldopa, labetalol, nifedipine, heparin, paracetamol, folic acid.","FDA categories (A, B, C, D, X) replaced by narrative PLLR; folic acid 400 µg prevents NTDs."],"Teratogen classics: 'Thalidomide Phocomelia, Valproate NTD, Warfarin nose, Lithium Ebstein'."],
"Teratogenicity is the capacity of a drug to cause fetal abnormalities when administered to a pregnant woman. Effects depend on the stage of gestation: pre-implantation (0–2 weeks after conception) — 'all-or-none' effect (death or normal survival); organogenesis/embryonic period (3–8 weeks; day 18–55) — maximum vulnerability to structural malformations; fetal period (after 8–9 weeks) — growth retardation, functional and neurobehavioural defects, organ toxicity, and late effects (DES — vaginal adenocarcinoma). Mechanisms: interference with cell division, folate antagonism, receptor-mediated effects (retinoids), oxidative stress, vascular disruption, endocrine disruption. Important human teratogens: thalidomide (phocomelia), isotretinoin and vitamin A analogues (craniofacial, CNS, cardiac defects), valproate (neural tube defects, cognitive impairment), carbamazepine (NTD), phenytoin (fetal hydantoin syndrome — craniofacial anomalies, digital hypoplasia), warfarin (fetal warfarin syndrome — nasal hypoplasia, stippled epiphyses; CNS bleed), ACE inhibitors and ARBs (renal agenesis, oligohydramnios in 2nd/3rd trimester), tetracyclines (teeth discoloration, bone), aminoglycosides (ototoxicity), methotrexate and other cytotoxics, lithium (Ebstein anomaly), diethylstilboestrol, androgens (virilisation of female fetus), misoprostol (Möbius sequence), statins, alcohol (fetal alcohol syndrome), mycophenolate, thalidomide analogues. Drugs considered relatively safe: penicillins, cephalosporins, erythromycin/azithromycin, insulin, metformin (with care), methyldopa, labetalol, nifedipine, heparin/LMWH, paracetamol, levothyroxine, folic acid, iron, chloroquine. Risk classification: earlier US FDA categories A, B, C, D, X (X — contraindicated: isotretinoin, thalidomide, statins, misoprostol, methotrexate, warfarin) replaced in 2015 by the Pregnancy and Lactation Labelling Rule (narrative risk summaries). Prevention: avoid drugs in first trimester unless essential, use well-established safe drugs at lowest effective dose, preconception counselling, folic acid supplementation (400 µg; 4–5 mg with prior NTD or antiepileptics), pregnancy testing before isotretinoin (iPLEDGE), registries.",
[["Teratogens and defects",[["Drug","Defect"],["Thalidomide","Phocomelia"],["Isotretinoin","Craniofacial, CNS, cardiac"],["Valproate, carbamazepine","Neural tube defects"],["Phenytoin","Fetal hydantoin syndrome"],["Warfarin","Nasal hypoplasia, stippled epiphyses"],["ACE inhibitors/ARBs","Renal dysgenesis, oligohydramnios"],["Tetracyclines","Teeth staining, bone"],["Lithium","Ebstein anomaly"],["DES","Vaginal clear cell adenocarcinoma"],["Alcohol","Fetal alcohol syndrome"],["Misoprostol","Möbius sequence"]]],
["Periods of sensitivity",["0–2 weeks: all-or-none","3–8 weeks: organogenesis — malformations","> 8 weeks: growth and functional defects"]]],
{c:"Drug safety in pregnancy depends on timing and agent; avoiding known teratogens, especially in the first trimester, and folic acid supplementation prevent many birth defects."});

Z("pharm1",16,"ph",["Drug dependence is a state in which a person needs repeated doses of a drug to feel normal (psychological dependence) or to avoid withdrawal symptoms (physical dependence).","Psychological dependence is a craving for the reward; physical dependence is the body having rewired itself so that stopping causes withdrawal.",["Psychological dependence: compulsive craving, drug-seeking (cocaine, cannabis, amphetamine, nicotine).","Physical dependence: adaptation → withdrawal syndrome on stopping (opioids, alcohol, barbiturates, benzodiazepines).","Addiction: compulsive use despite harm, loss of control; involves mesolimbic dopamine reward pathway.","Withdrawal: opioids — yawning, lacrimation, rhinorrhoea, piloerection, diarrhoea (not fatal); alcohol/barbiturates — seizures, delirium tremens (can be fatal). Treatment: detoxification (methadone, buprenorphine, benzodiazepines), maintenance, naltrexone, counselling."],"Fatal withdrawal: 'Alcohol, Barbiturates, Benzodiazepines (sedatives)' — not opioids."],
"Drug dependence (WHO) is a state, psychic and sometimes also physical, resulting from interaction between a living organism and a drug, characterised by behavioural and other responses that always include a compulsion to take the drug on a continuous or periodic basis to experience its psychic effects and sometimes to avoid the discomfort of its absence. Psychological dependence: the individual believes that optimal well-being is achieved only through the drug's actions; craving and drug-seeking behaviour (cocaine, amphetamines, cannabis, nicotine, LSD). Physical dependence: altered physiological state from prolonged use, in which abrupt discontinuation produces a characteristic withdrawal (abstinence) syndrome, often opposite to the drug's effects (opioids, alcohol, barbiturates, benzodiazepines, nicotine). Related terms: drug abuse/misuse (non-medical use harmful to health), addiction (compulsive use with loss of control and continued use despite harm; substance use disorder in DSM-5), habituation (less intense involvement), tolerance. Neurobiology: most drugs of abuse increase dopamine in the mesolimbic reward pathway (ventral tegmental area → nucleus accumbens); neuroadaptations underlie tolerance, dependence and craving. Withdrawal syndromes: opioids — lacrimation, rhinorrhoea, yawning, sweating, mydriasis, piloerection (cold turkey), abdominal cramps, diarrhoea, anxiety; uncomfortable but rarely fatal; alcohol — tremors, anxiety, seizures, hallucinosis, delirium tremens (potentially fatal); barbiturates and benzodiazepines — anxiety, insomnia, seizures, delirium; nicotine — irritability, craving, weight gain; cocaine/amphetamine — depression, fatigue, hypersomnia, craving. Management: detoxification with substitution and tapering (methadone or buprenorphine for opioids, chlordiazepoxide/diazepam for alcohol, clonidine for autonomic symptoms), maintenance therapy (methadone, buprenorphine, opioid substitution therapy), relapse prevention (naltrexone, acamprosate, disulfiram for alcohol; varenicline, bupropion, NRT for nicotine), psychosocial interventions, rehabilitation; India — NDPS Act, Nasha Mukt Bharat Abhiyaan.",
[["Psychological vs physical dependence",[["Feature","Psychological","Physical"],["Basis","Craving for euphoria","Neuroadaptation"],["Withdrawal syndrome","No/mild","Yes"],["Examples","Cocaine, cannabis, amphetamine","Opioids, alcohol, barbiturates, benzodiazepines"]]],
["Withdrawal and treatment",[["Drug","Withdrawal","Treatment"],["Opioids","Lacrimation, yawning, piloerection, diarrhoea","Methadone, buprenorphine, clonidine"],["Alcohol","Tremor, seizures, delirium tremens","Benzodiazepines, thiamine"],["Benzodiazepines","Anxiety, seizures","Slow taper"],["Nicotine","Irritability, craving","NRT, varenicline, bupropion"]]]],
{c:"Drug dependence involves psychological craving and physical neuroadaptation; recognising withdrawal patterns guides safe detoxification and long-term rehabilitation."});

Z("pharm1",17,"ph",["Pharmacovigilance is the science and activities of detecting, assessing, understanding and preventing adverse effects of medicines after they reach the market.","Clinical trials test a drug on thousands; pharmacovigilance keeps watching it in millions of real patients to catch rare or delayed harms.",["Why: trials are small, short, exclude children, elderly, pregnant women; rare ADRs (1 in 10,000) missed.","Methods: spontaneous reporting (most common), active surveillance, cohort and case-control studies, prescription event monitoring, registries.","India: Pharmacovigilance Programme of India (PvPI, IPC Ghaziabad, 2010); report through ADR forms, AMCs, toll-free 1800-180-3024, ADR PvPI app; VigiBase (WHO Uppsala).","Causality: WHO-UMC scale, Naranjo algorithm; outcomes — label changes, warnings, withdrawals (rofecoxib, cisapride)."],"PvPI: 'Report suspected ADR — any health professional, even patients'."],
"Pharmacovigilance (WHO) is the science and activities relating to the detection, assessment, understanding and prevention of adverse effects or any other drug-related problems. Need: pre-marketing clinical trials involve limited numbers (few thousand), short duration and selected populations, so rare, delayed and population-specific adverse drug reactions (ADRs), drug interactions and long-term effects are detected only after marketing (phase IV). Objectives: early detection of new ADRs and interactions, estimation of frequency, identification of risk factors, risk–benefit assessment, communication of information, promotion of rational and safe use, and regulatory action. Methods: passive surveillance — spontaneous reporting of suspected ADRs by health professionals and patients (yellow card in UK, MedWatch in USA, Suspected ADR Reporting Form in India); stimulated reporting; active surveillance — sentinel sites, prescription event monitoring, cohort event monitoring, registries, targeted clinical investigations; comparative observational studies (cohort, case-control); data mining of large databases and signal detection. India: Pharmacovigilance Programme of India launched in 2010 by CDSCO with Indian Pharmacopoeia Commission (Ghaziabad) as National Coordination Centre; network of ADR Monitoring Centres in medical colleges; reports uploaded to VigiFlow and shared with WHO Uppsala Monitoring Centre (VigiBase); helpline 1800-180-3024 and ADR PvPI mobile app; Materiovigilance and Haemovigilance programmes. Causality assessment: WHO-UMC system (certain, probable, possible, unlikely, conditional, unassessable), Naranjo algorithm; severity (Hartwig scale), preventability (Schumock–Thornton). Outcomes: signals leading to label changes, boxed warnings, restricted use, dear doctor letters, or withdrawal (thalidomide, rofecoxib, cisapride, phenylpropanolamine, nimesulide in children, analgin in some countries). Role of doctors: recognise and report every suspected ADR, especially serious, unexpected ones and those with new drugs.",
[["Methods",[["Method","Features"],["Spontaneous reporting","Simple, cheap; under-reporting"],["Active surveillance","Sentinel sites, cohort event monitoring"],["Cohort/case-control studies","Quantify risk"],["Registries","Pregnancy, rare diseases"],["Data mining","Signal detection from databases"]]],
["PvPI",["Started 2010; NCC — IPC Ghaziabad","AMCs in medical colleges","Suspected ADR form; helpline 1800-180-3024; ADR PvPI app","Data to WHO-UMC VigiBase"]]],
{c:"Pharmacovigilance continuously monitors drug safety after marketing; every clinician's reporting of suspected ADRs strengthens patient safety."});

Z("pharm1",18,"ph",["Drug interactions occur when one drug alters the effect of another — through changes in absorption, distribution, metabolism or excretion (pharmacokinetic) or at the site of action (pharmacodynamic).","Two drugs in the body can help each other, block each other, or one can change how the other is handled — sometimes dangerously (warfarin + NSAIDs → bleeding).",["Absorption: tetracycline + antacids/iron (chelation); levodopa + atropine (delayed gastric emptying ↓ levodopa absorption as more is degraded in gut).","Distribution: displacement from protein binding (warfarin by aspirin; bilirubin by sulphonamides).","Metabolism: inducers (rifampicin ↓ OCP, warfarin) and inhibitors (erythromycin, ketoconazole ↑ terfenadine/cisapride → torsades; cimetidine ↑ warfarin).","Excretion: probenecid ↓ penicillin secretion (useful); pharmacodynamic — warfarin + NSAIDs (antiplatelet + GI ulcer + displacement → bleeding), sildenafil + nitrates (hypotension), MAOI + tyramine (hypertensive crisis)."],"Interactions 'A-D-M-E + Dynamic'."],
"A drug interaction is the modification of the action of one drug by another (or by food, herbs, alcohol) when given together or in sequence. Interactions may be beneficial (levodopa + carbidopa; amoxicillin + clavulanate; probenecid + penicillin; naloxone for opioid overdose) or harmful. Pharmacokinetic interactions: absorption — chelation (tetracyclines and fluoroquinolones with antacids, iron, calcium, milk), altered gastric pH (ketoconazole needs acid; PPIs reduce absorption), altered gastric emptying (atropine and other anticholinergics delay gastric emptying, so levodopa remains longer in the stomach and is degraded, reducing its absorption; metoclopramide speeds absorption), adsorption (cholestyramine binds digoxin, warfarin), P-glycoprotein effects; distribution — displacement from plasma protein binding (aspirin, NSAIDs, sulphonamides displacing warfarin, sulphonylureas, methotrexate; sulphonamides displacing bilirubin in neonates); metabolism — enzyme induction (rifampicin, phenytoin, carbamazepine reduce efficacy of OCPs, warfarin, steroids, antiretrovirals) and inhibition (erythromycin, clarithromycin, ketoconazole, cimetidine, ciprofloxacin, valproate increase levels of warfarin, theophylline, phenytoin, statins, cisapride/terfenadine → QT prolongation, torsades; allopurinol inhibits xanthine oxidase → azathioprine/6-MP toxicity; MAO inhibitors + tyramine-rich food → hypertensive crisis); excretion — competition for tubular secretion (probenecid prolongs penicillin; aspirin reduces methotrexate excretion), altered urine pH (alkalinisation enhances salicylate and phenobarbitone excretion), diuretics reduce lithium clearance (toxicity). Pharmacodynamic interactions: synergism (additive — alcohol + benzodiazepines CNS depression; potentiation — sulphamethoxazole + trimethoprim), antagonism (naloxone vs morphine, β-agonists vs β-blockers), warfarin + NSAIDs/aspirin (impaired platelet function, GI ulceration and protein displacement → serious bleeding), sildenafil + nitrates (severe hypotension), ACE inhibitors + potassium-sparing diuretics (hyperkalaemia), QT-prolonging drug combinations, serotonin syndrome (SSRIs + MAOIs, tramadol), digoxin + diuretics (hypokalaemia → digoxin toxicity), aminoglycosides + loop diuretics (ototoxicity). Prevention: limit polypharmacy, check interaction resources, monitor narrow-therapeutic-index drugs (warfarin INR, lithium, digoxin), adjust doses.",
[["Examples",[["Mechanism","Interaction","Result"],["Absorption","Atropine + levodopa","↓ Levodopa effect (delayed gastric emptying)"],["Absorption","Tetracycline + antacids","Chelation, ↓ absorption"],["Distribution","Aspirin + warfarin","Displacement, bleeding"],["Metabolism (induction)","Rifampicin + OCP","Contraceptive failure"],["Metabolism (inhibition)","Erythromycin/ketoconazole + terfenadine","QT prolongation, torsades"],["Excretion","Probenecid + penicillin","Prolonged penicillin levels (beneficial)"],["Pharmacodynamic","Warfarin + NSAIDs","GI bleeding"],["Pharmacodynamic","Sildenafil + nitrates","Severe hypotension"],["Pharmacodynamic","MAOI + tyramine","Hypertensive crisis"]]]],
{c:"Drug interactions are common and often predictable; awareness of mechanisms, careful prescribing and monitoring of narrow-therapeutic-index drugs prevent harm."});
